 Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 locally irregular subgraphs // Restore the saved color scheme preference, or // enact the browser preference if "automatic", // without expecting DOM load to have completed. // // Also restore any saved readingmode and ToC display preferences. function initializeReadingPreferences() { let saved_theme = localStorage.getItem("ar5iv_theme") || "automatic"; if (saved_theme === "automatic") { if (window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches) { saved_theme = "dark"; } } if (saved_theme == "dark") { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", "dark"); } else { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", "light"); } const tocDisplay = localStorage.getItem('arxiv_html_paper_toc_display'); if (tocDisplay) { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-toc-display", tocDisplay); } const readingMode = localStorage.getItem('arxiv_html_paper_reading_mode'); if (readingMode) { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-reading-mode", readingMode); } } // Run as soon as JS starts, to minimize repainting initializeReadingPreferences(); /* Reading mode hides the chrome and the empty .arxiv-html-header marker: the pinned JS reads the marker's display to know which way to toggle. The marker draws no box. */ .arxiv-html-header { display: contents; } html[data-reading-mode="enabled"] :is(.arxiv-html-site-chrome, .arxiv-html-header, .ds-site-footer) { display: none; } /* The paper controls' icons are blocks, so they sit centred (the pinned JS shows the theme icon by making its span a block). */ .arxiv-html-site-chrome .ds-site-header-tools svg { display: block; width: 18px; height: 18px; flex-shrink: 0; fill: currentColor; } /* ar5iv dims every image in dark mode and puts it on a white wash, for paper figures; arXiv's own render as they are (the logo on the bar, the funder logos on their white chips). Important: ar5iv's dark rules are unlayered, as specific as :root:not([data-theme]) img:not(.ltx_no_dark_filter). */ :is(.ds-site-header, .ds-site-footer) img { filter: none !important; } .ds-site-header img { background-color: transparent !important; } /* Wide (the theme's 1280px): the theme lays the body out as a grid whose full-width top rows were the band's and its sticky reader bar's. The band takes the first and scrolls away; the header, the paper's bar (no site navigation, the paper's controls), takes the second and stays in view, the one sticky bar, with the anchor clearance that requires (DESIGN-POLICIES). The controls show their names. */ @media (min-width: 1280px) { html { scroll-padding-top: 80px; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome { display: contents; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome > #ds-announcement { grid-area: announce; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome > .ds-site-header { grid-area: header; position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 3; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-icon { display: none; } } /* Narrow: the header scrolls away, and the controls show icons (names kept for screen readers; Why HTML? goes). The theme's contents panel is fixed below where its old sticky bar was, and hidden only once the reader has closed it, so until then it is an invisible box over the header and the paper: it shows only while open, under the chrome, in its place in the page. The paper's footer (build logs, reporting instructions) keeps the text's side inset; the theme gives it none, so it met the screen edges. */ @media (max-width: 1279px) { .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-wide { display: none; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-label { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); white-space: nowrap; } html:not([data-toc-display="block"]) .ltx_page_navbar { display: none; } .ltx_page_navbar { position: absolute; top: auto; } .arxiv-html-footer { padding-inline: 1rem; } } Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Submit without GitHub Submit in GitHub Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme Abstract. 1 Introduction 2 Outline of the main ideas 3 Tools 4 Proof of Theorem 4.1 Random colour assignment and distinguished sets 4.2 Assuring desired features of the distinguished sets 4.3 Decomposition of G G to two similar subgraphs 4.4 A decomposition of H 0 H_{0} to two locally irregular subgraphs 4.5 Justification of no degree conflicts in F 1 F_{1} and F 2 F_{2} 5 Final remarks References License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license arXiv:2402.18739v1 [math.CO] 28 Feb 2024 Decomposability of regular graphs to 4 4 locally irregular subgraphs Jakub Przybyło Address: AGH University of Krakow, al. A. Mickiewicza 30, 30-059 Krakow, Poland Email address: jakubprz@agh.edu.pl Abstract. A locally irregular graph is a graph whose adjacent vertices have distinct degrees. It was conjectured that every connected graph is edge decomposable to 3 3 locally irregular subgraphs, unless it belongs to a certain family of exceptions, including graphs of small maximum degrees, which are not decomposable to any number of such subgraphs. Recently Sedlar and Škrekovski exhibited a counterexample to the conjecture, which necessitates a decomposition to (at least) 4 4 locally irregular subgraphs. We prove that every d d -regular graph with d d large enough, i.e. d ≥ 54000 d\geq 54000 , is decomposable to 4 4 locally irregular subgraphs. Our proof relies on a mixture of a numerically optimized application of the probabilistic method and certain deterministic results on degree constrained subgraphs due to Addario-Berry, Dalal, McDiarmid, Reed, and Thomason, and to Alon and Wei, introduced in the context of related problems concerning irregular subgraphs. Key words and phrases: locally irregular graph, graph decomposition, edge set partition 1. Introduction It is one of the most basic observations in graph theory that no simple, finite graph G G with at least 2 2 vertices can be completely irregular, i.e. its vertices cannot have all pairwise distinct degrees. Some alternative concepts of irregular graphs were thus investigated already by Chartrand, Erdős and Oellermann [ 12 ] , and others, see e.g. [ 6 , 7 ] . More recently, a notion of a locally irregular graph was introduced in [ 8 ] . It is a graph G = ( V , E ) G=(V,E) whose adjacent vertices have distinct degrees, i.e. d G ​ ( u ) ≠ d G ​ ( v ) d_{G}(u)\neq d_{G}(v) for every edge u ​ v ∈ E uv\in E . In [ 8 ] also a problem of edge decomposability of graphs to locally irregular subgraphs was raised. We say that a graph G = ( V , E ) G=(V,E) is decomposable to k k locally irregular subgraphs if its edge set can be partitioned to k k subsets: E = E 1 ∪ E 2 ∪ … ∪ E k E=E_{1}\cup E_{2}\cup\ldots\cup E_{k} such that the subgraph G i = ( V , E i ) G_{i}=(V,E_{i}) is locally irregular for i = 1 , 2 , … , k i=1,2,\ldots,k . This concept is related with a recent direction of research of Alon and Wei [ 5 ] , also developed by Fox, Luo and Pham [ 15 ] , who investigated so-called irregular subgraphs . See [ 21 ] for discussion and results linking the two concepts. The notion of locally irregular graphs is also strongly related with yet another well-known descendant of research on irregular graphs [ 12 ] and so-called irregularity strength of graphs, see e.g. [ 13 ] , which is the 1–2–3 Conjecture . This was posed in 2004 by Karoński, Łuczak and Thomason [ 16 ] and recently confirmed by Keusch [ 17 ] . It may be phrased as follows. Given any connected graph with at least 3 3 vertices, one may multiply every its edge e e to at most 3 3 copies of e e so that the resulting multigraph is locally irregular, i.e. its adjacent vertices have pairwise distinct degrees. See e.g. [ 8 ] for a more comprehensive discussion on relations between these concepts. Also in [ 8 ] a family 𝔗 ′ \mathfrak{T}^{\prime} of all connected graphs which are not decomposable to any number of locally irregular subgraphs was identified. The central initial problem in the study devoted to locally irregular graphs was the following conjecture. Conjecture 1 ( [ 8 ] ) . Each connected graph G G which does not belong to 𝔗 ′ \mathfrak{T}^{\prime} is decomposable to 3 3 locally irregular subgraphs. It is worth mentioning that the family 𝔗 ′ \mathfrak{T}^{\prime} includes only graphs with maximum degree upper bounded by 3 3 . It thus was natural to investigate wether graphs with sufficiently large degrees are decomposable to 3 3 locally irregular subgraphs. In [ 8 ] this was in particular proven to hold for regular graphs, which are in some sense as far from being locally irregular as possible. Theorem 2 ( [ 8 ] ) . Each d d -regular graph with d ≥ 10 7 d\geq 10^{7} is decomposable to 3 3 locally irregular subgraphs. This result was further extended towards all graphs G G with minimum degree δ ⁡ ( G ) ≥ 10 10 \delta(G)\geq 10^{10} in [ 22 ] . Exploiting in particular this theorem, Bensmail, Merker and Thomassen [ 11 ] proved next that each connected graph outside 𝔗 ′ \mathfrak{T}^{\prime} is decomposable to at most 328 328 locally irregular subgraphs. The bound of 328 328 was also latter refined to 220 220 in [ 20 ] , see e.g. [ 8 , 9 , 10 , 18 , 19 , 20 , 23 ] for other related results concerning locally irregular graphs. Surprisingly, quite recently a single connected exception to Conjecture 1 was found by Sedlar and Škrekovski [ 24 ] . This is decomposable to 4 4 locally irregular subgraphs. Consequently, they posed the following less restrictive variant of the conjecture. Conjecture 3 ( [ 24 ] ) . Each connected graph G G which does not belong to 𝔗 ′ \mathfrak{T}^{\prime} is decomposable to 4 4 locally irregular subgraphs. As the main contribution of this paper we provide the following significant reduction of the upper bound 10 7 10^{7} from Theorem 2 in context of the new Conjecture 3 . In a way this concerns the most challenging in face of our goals family of graphs, which are extremely far from being (locally) irregular. Theorem 4 . Each d d -regular graph with d ≥ 54000 d\geq 54000 is decomposable to 4 4 locally irregular subgraphs. Let us remark that our approach is significantly different from the one used in [ 8 ] to prove Theorem 2 . It benefits in particular, but not exclusively, from the allowed one more locally irregular subgraph, which admitted an entirely new design of a desired decomposition. Its first phase exploits the probabilistic method in order to initially decompose a given regular graph to 2 2 subgraphs with relatively small chromatic numbers, where we put some effort to optimize our approach with regard to minimizing the lower bound for d d in Theorem 4 . In the second, deterministic part of our argument we also make use of results stemming from the mentioned related problems, i.e. certain theorems on degree constrained subgraphs of Addario-Berry, Dalal, McDiarmid, Reed, and Thomason [ 1 ] , and of Alon and Wei [ 5 ] . We believe the applied strategy is quite interesting by itself. In the second section we shall outline the main ideas of the proof of Theorem 4 . Next we shall present several useful tools. Section 4 contains the complete proof of our main result. The last section includes concluding remarks. 2. Outline of the main ideas The main vague idea of the proof of Theorem 4 is the following. In the beginning we shall partition the edges of a given d d -regular graph G = ( V , E ) G=(V,E) to two subsets. Each of these shall induce a graph H i H_{i} , i = 0 , 1 i=0,1 , with several identical features. First of all each H i H_{i} shall be close to a d 2 \frac{d}{2} -regular graph, but shall have relatively small chromatic number. For this purpose we shall randomly associate to every vertex v ∈ V v\in V a pair of integers ( O v , I v ) ∈ [ K ] 2 (O_{v},I_{v})\in[K]^{2} , called its colour, where K K is small compared to d d and [ K ] = { 1 , 2 , … , K } [K]=\{1,2,\ldots,K\} . Than we shall colour the edges accordingly with 0 0 and 1 1 , following several rules – the edges coloured 0 0 shall induce H 0 H_{0} and the ones coloured 1 1 shall form H 1 H_{1} . The values O v O_{v} shall in fact associate the vertices with independent sets in H 0 H_{0} (witnessing its relatively small chromatic number) and the values I v I_{v} shall play the same role for H 1 H_{1} . In order to maintain a better error control, the aforementioned 0 0 - 1 1 -edge partition shall be partly performed deterministically, using Lemma 9 of Alon and Wei. More specifically, the random vertex colours assignment shall be mainly used to provide large independent sets in H 0 H_{0} and H 1 H_{1} , while the rest of the required features of H 0 H_{0} , H 1 H_{1} shall be assured due to Lemma 9 9 , providing almost perfectly equal distribution of colours 0 0 and 1 1 in certain distinguished subsets of edges. Then each of the graphs H i H_{i} , i = 0 , 1 i=0,1 , shall be further decomposed into two subgraphs, say F 1 F_{1} and F 2 F_{2} . These, unlike H 0 H_{0} and H 1 H_{1} , shall have different sets of properties and features, which in varied ways shall imply that F 1 F_{1} , F 2 F_{2} are locally irregular. The degrees in F 1 F_{1} shall be determined by O v O_{v} or I v I_{v} . More precisely, these shall be (almost) equal to twice one of these values modulo 2 ​ K 2K , and thus shall be different for adjacent vertices from different independent sets mentioned above. In order to construct such F 1 F_{1} we shall use Lemma 8 , concerning existence in every graph very restrictively degree constrained subgraphs, which essentially was developed by Addario-Berry, Dalal, McDiarmid, Reed and Thomason while conducting research over the 1–2–3 Conjecture. In order to assure that the complement of F 1 F_{1} in H i H_{i} , that is F 2 F_{2} , is locally irregular as well, we shall on the other hand first identify potentially risky edges from the point of view of F 2 F_{2} . All of these shall be included in F 1 F_{1} prior to using Lemma 8 , which shall allow us to avoid degree conflicts in F 2 F_{2} . As the values ( O v , I v ) (O_{v},I_{v}) shall be chosen randomly and independently for every v ∈ V v\in V , some pairs of adjacent vertices shall receive identical colours. If this occurs we shall uncolour both of them. We shall however show that the set U U of such uncolored vertices is relatively sparse, and thus we shall be able to handle them separately, using a natural greedy approach. In fact these vertices shall be dealt with before the remaining ones. 3. Tools We shall use several tools useful while applying the probabilistic method. In particular some concentration inequalities: the well-known Chernoff Bound and the McDiarmid’s Inequality, cf. e.g. [ 14 ] . Theorem 5 ( Chernoff Bound ) . Let X 1 , X 2 , … , X n X_{1},X_{2},\ldots,X_{n} be independent random variables taking values in { 0 , 1 } \{0,1\} . Let X = ∑ i = 1 n X i X=\sum_{i=1}^{n}X_{i} and μ = 𝔼 ⁡ ( X ) \mu=\mathbb{E}(X) . Then for all δ ≥ 0 \delta\geq 0 we have ℙ ⁡ ( X ≥ ( 1 + δ ) ​ μ ) ≤ ( e δ ( 1 + δ ) 1 + δ ) μ . \mathbb{P}\left(X\geq(1+\delta)\mu\right)\leq\left(\frac{e^{\delta}}{(1+\delta)^{1+\delta}}\right)^{\mu}. Note the same upper bound holds if we only know that μ ≥ 𝔼 ⁡ ( X ) \mu\geq\mathbb{E}(X) . Theorem 6 ( McDiarmid’s Inequality ) . Let Z = Z ⁡ ( W 1 , W 2 , … , W n ) Z=Z(W_{1},W_{2},\ldots,W_{n}) be a random variable that depends on n n independent random variables W 1 , W 2 , W_{1},W_{2}, … , \ldots, W n W_{n} . Suppose that | Z ⁡ ( W 1 , … , W i , … , W n ) − Z ⁡ ( W 1 , … , W i ′ , … , W n ) | ≤ c i |Z(W_{1},\ldots,W_{i},\ldots,W_{n})-Z(W_{1},\ldots,W^{\prime}_{i},\ldots,W_{n})|\leq c_{i} for all i = 1 , 2 , … , n i=1,2,\ldots,n and W 1 , W 2 , … , W n , W i ′ W_{1},W_{2},\ldots,W_{n},W^{\prime}_{i} . Then for all t > 0 t>0 we have ℙ ⁡ ( Z ≥ 𝔼 ⁡ ( Z ) + t ) ≤ e − t 2 2 ​ ∑ i = 1 n c i 2 . \mathbb{P}\left(Z\geq\mathbb{E}(Z)+t\right)\leq e^{-\frac{t^{2}}{2\sum_{i=1}^{n}c_{i}^{2}}}. We shall also need a variant of the symmetric Lovász Local Lemma, involving conditional probabilities of bad events, cf. e.g. [ 4 ] (Corollary 5.1.2 and comments beneath its proof). Theorem 7 ( Lovász Local Lemma ) . Let 𝒜 = { A 1 , A 2 , … , A n } \mathcal{A}=\{A_{1},A_{2},\ldots,A_{n}\} be a family of events in an arbitrary probability space. Suppose that for every i ∈ [ n ] i\in[n] there is a set Γ ⁡ ( A i ) ⊂ 𝒜 \Gamma(A_{i})\subset\mathcal{A} of size at most D D such that for each ℬ ⊆ 𝒜 ∖ ( Γ ⁡ ( A i ) ∪ { A i } ) \mathcal{B}\subseteq\mathcal{A}\smallsetminus(\Gamma(A_{i})\cup\{A_{i}\}) : ℙ ⁡ ( A i | ⋂ B ∈ ℬ B ¯ ) ≤ 1 e ⁡ ( D + 1 ) . \mathbb{P}\left(A_{i}|\bigcap_{B\in\mathcal{B}}\overline{B}\right)\leq\frac{1}{e(D+1)}. Then ℙ ⁡ ( ⋂ i = 1 n A i ¯ ) > 0 \mathbb{P}\left(\bigcap_{i=1}^{n}\overline{A_{i}}\right)>0 . For the deterministic part of our proof we shall also need the following lemma from [ 22 ] (Corollary 9), concerning degree constrained subgraph of a given graph, which is a direct consequence of Theorem 2.2 due to Addario-Berry, Dalal, McDiarmid, Reed and Thomason from [ 1 ] (see also [ 2 , 3 ] for similar degree theorems and their applications). Lemma 8 . Suppose that for some graph G = ( V , E ) G=(V,E) with minimum degree at least 12 12 we have chosen, for every vertex v v , an integer λ v ≥ 2 \lambda_{v}\geq 2 with 6 ​ λ v ≤ d ⁡ ( v ) 6\lambda_{v}\leq d(v) . Then for every assignment t : V → ℤ , t:V\to\mathbb{Z}, there exists a spanning subgraph H H of G G such that d H ​ ( v ) ∈ [ d ⁡ ( v ) 3 , 2 ​ d ​ ( v ) 3 ] d_{H}(v)\in[\frac{d(v)}{3},\frac{2d(v)}{3}] and d H ​ ( v ) ≡ t ⁡ ( v ) ( mod λ v ) d_{H}(v)\equiv t(v)\pmod{\lambda_{v}} or d H ​ ( v ) ≡ t ⁡ ( v ) + 1 ( mod λ v ) d_{H}(v)\equiv t(v)+1\pmod{\lambda_{v}} for each v ∈ V v\in V . We shall also use the following lemma due to Alon and Wei [ 5 ] (see Lemma 4.1). Lemma 9 . Let G = ( V , E ) G=(V,E) be a graph, and let z : E → [ 0 , 1 ] z:E\to[0,1] be a weight function assigning to each edge e ∈ E e\in E a real weight z ⁡ ( e ) z(e) in [ 0 , 1 ] [0,1] . Then there is a function x : E → { 0 , 1 } x:E\to\{0,1\} assigning to each edge an integer value in { 0 , 1 } \{0,1\} so that for every v ∈ V v\in V ∑ e ∋ v z ⁡ ( e ) − 1 < ∑ e ∋ v x ⁡ ( e ) ≤ ∑ e ∋ v z ⁡ ( e ) + 1 . \sum_{e\ni v}z(e)-1<\sum_{e\ni v}x(e)\leq\sum_{e\ni v}z(e)+1. Let G = ( V , E ) G=(V,E) be a graph and let v , w ∈ V v,w\in V , V ′ ⊆ V V^{\prime}\subseteq V , E ′ ⊆ E E^{\prime}\subseteq E . By N G ​ ( v ) N_{G}(v) we understand the set of neighbours of v v in G G . We denote by E ′ ​ ( v ) E^{\prime}(v) the set of edges in E ′ E^{\prime} incident with v v . We also define d E ′ ​ ( v ) = | E ′ ​ ( v ) | d_{E^{\prime}}(v)=|E^{\prime}(v)| , d V ′ ​ ( v ) = | N G ​ ( v ) ∩ V ′ | d_{V^{\prime}}(v)=|N_{G}(v)\cap V^{\prime}| . Finally, by dist G ​ ( v , w ) {\rm dist}_{G}(v,w) we denote the distance between v v and w w in G G . 4. Proof of Theorem 4 4.1. Random colour assignment and distinguished sets Suppose G G is a d d -regular graph with d ≥ 54000 d\geq 54000 . Let us fix a few optimized constants: (4.1) k = 0.025 , s = 0.0031 , r = 0.26 , u = 0.131 . k=0.025,\hskip 14.22636pts=0.0031,\hskip 14.22636ptr=0.26,\hskip 14.22636ptu=0.131. Set K = ⌈ k ​ d ⌉ . K=\left\lceil kd\right\rceil. Let us randomly assign to every vertex v ∈ V v\in V a colour C v = ( O v , I v ) ∈ [ K ] 2 C_{v}=(O_{v},I_{v})\in[K]^{2} , i.e. O v , I v O_{v},I_{v} , v ∈ V v\in V , are independent random variables uniformly distributed over [ K ] [K] . Denote the set of vertices to be uncoloured by U = { v ∈ V | ∃ w ∈ N G ( v ) : C w = C v } . U=\{v\in V~|~\exists w\in N_{G}(v):C_{w}=C_{v}\}. and let U e U^{e} be the set of edges induced by U U , U e = { v w ∈ E : v , w ∈ U } . U^{e}=\{vw\in E:v,w\in U\}. We further distinguish the set of edges ,, touching ” U U : T = { v ​ w ∈ E : v ∈ U ∨ w ∈ U } T=\{vw\in E:v\in U\vee w\in U\} and the set of special edges: S = { v ​ w ∈ E ∖ T : O v = O w ∨ I v = I w } . S=\{vw\in E\smallsetminus T:O_{v}=O_{w}\vee I_{v}=I_{w}\}. We also define a certain set of risky edges , where by | m − n | K |m-n|_{K} we understand the distance between integers m m and n n modulo K K , i.e. | m − n | K = min ⁡ { m − n ⁡ ( mod ​ K ) , n − m ⁡ ( mod ​ K ) } |m-n|_{K}=\min\{m-n~({\rm mod}~K),~n-m~({\rm mod}~K)\} : R = { v ​ w ∈ E ∖ T : 1 ≤ | O v − O w | K ≤ s ​ d + 7 2 ∨ 1 ≤ | I v − I w | K ≤ s ​ d + 7 2 } R=\left\{vw\in E\smallsetminus T:1\leq|O_{v}-O_{w}|_{K}\leq\frac{sd+7}{2}\vee 1\leq|I_{v}-I_{w}|_{K}\leq\frac{sd+7}{2}\right\} and the set of edges which are risky but not special: R ′ = R ∖ S . R^{\prime}=R\smallsetminus S. Finally, we define two more specific subsets of edges: E ′ = E ∖ ( T ∪ R ) , E ′′ = E ∖ ( T ∪ R ∪ S ) . E^{\prime}=E\smallsetminus(T\cup R),\hskip 28.45274ptE^{\prime\prime}=E\smallsetminus(T\cup R\cup S). We shall first prove that we may choose such a vertex colours assignment so that each vertex v v of G G is adjacent with bounded numbers of vertices of certain types. For clarity it shall usually be more convenient to refer further on to the corresponding edges, joining a given vertex v v with vertices of some types. The only exception adheres to d U ​ ( v ) d_{U}(v) , which shall always mean the number of neighbours w ∈ U w\in U of v v such that v ​ w ∈ E vw\in E (i.e. shall always refer to the neighbourhood of v v in the entire G G ). 4.2. Assuring desired features of the distinguished sets Claim 10 . With positive probability, for every v ∈ V v\in V : (i) d S ​ ( v ) < s ​ d d_{S}(v)<sd , (ii) d R ​ ( v ) < r ​ d d_{R}(v)<rd , (iii) d U ​ ( v ) < u ​ d d_{U}(v)<ud . Proof. For every v ∈ V v\in V , let us denote the events: • S v : d S ​ ( v ) ≥ s ​ d S_{v}:~d_{S}(v)\geq sd ; • R v : d R ​ ( v ) ≥ r ​ d R_{v}:~d_{R}(v)\geq rd ; • U v : d U ​ ( v ) ≥ u ​ d U_{v}:~d_{U}(v)\geq ud . Let 𝒜 \mathcal{A} be the set of all such events. For every v v and A v ∈ { S v , R v , U v } A_{v}\in\{S_{v},R_{v},U_{v}\} we define: (4.2) Γ ( A v ) = ⋃ w : dist G ​ ( v , w ) ≤ 3 { S w , R w , U w } ∖ { A v } . \Gamma(A_{v})=\bigcup_{w:~{\rm dist}_{G}(v,w)\leq 3}\{S_{w},R